Instruction: Read the segment of a merger agreement and answer the multiple-choice question by choosing the option that best characterizes the agreement.\nQuestion: Is "financial point of view" the sole consideration when determining whether an offer is superior?\nOption A: No\nOption B: Yes\n\nMerger Agreement: SECTION 9.4. Evaluation of Acquisition Proposals. <omitted> (c) Notwithstanding any other provision of this Agreement, during the Pre-Closing Period, the Company Board may effect a Company Adverse Recommendation Change if, and only if, (i) the Company Board determines in good faith, after consultation with the Company's outside legal counsel and financial advisors, that the failure to make the Company Adverse Recommendation Change would be inconsistent with the fiduciary duties of the Company Board under applicable Law, (ii) the Company has provided Parent with a written notice of the Company Board's intention to make a Company Adverse Recommendation Change at least four Business Days prior to taking such action, and (iii) the Company Board has determined that the Acquisition Proposal constitutes a Superior Proposal after considering all relevant factors, including potential regulatory approvals and risk assessments, and after consultation with its legal and financial advisors. The Company shall negotiate in good faith with Parent during the four Business Day period to consider any adjustments proposed by Parent to the terms and conditions of this Agreement. If the Company Board continues to determine that the Acquisition Proposal is a Superior Proposal, it may proceed with the Company Adverse Recommendation Change in compliance with applicable fiduciary duties and legal obligations. (Pages 84-85)\nAnswer:
Choices:

