Instruction: Read the segment of a merger agreement and answer the multiple-choice question by choosing the option that best characterizes the agreement.\nQuestion: Is “financial point of view” the sole consideration when determining whether an offer is superior?\nOption A: No\nOption B: Yes\n\nMerger Agreement: SECTION 8.3. Evaluation of Acquisition Proposals. <omitted> (f) Notwithstanding anything in this Agreement, the Company Board may change its recommendation if: (i) the Company receives a bona fide written Acquisition Proposal that has not been withdrawn, (ii) the Company Board, after consultation with its outside legal counsel and a financial advisor of nationally recognized standing, determines in good faith that such Acquisition Proposal constitutes a Superior Proposal, and (iii) the Company has provided Parent prior written notice of its intent to change its recommendation, including all material terms of the Acquisition Proposal, at least three Business Days in advance. During such period, the Company shall negotiate in good faith with Parent to make adjustments to the terms of this Agreement so that the Acquisition Proposal would no longer constitute a Superior Proposal. If the Company Board still determines that the Acquisition Proposal is a Superior Proposal, after considering the results of such negotiations and consulting with its legal and financial advisors, it may proceed with the recommendation change. (Pages 82-83)\nAnswer: A
